prompt='''CRITICAL STATE CONTINUITY: The action requested or position described in the practice sentence MUST still be true/unresolved at practice. Never board, depart, close door, return home, pick up, give or move an object in scene4 then repeat that same unfinished action in5/6. For physical phrasal verbs, the practice comes BEFORE the physical transition, then the NEXT scene shows actual before/after (extra_visual_states literal quote if needed). Main camera stays coherent. No visual prose with code IDs. Keep technical props as background, no unverified mechanisms/material/medical/safety claims.\nCRITICAL STYLE: Personal short film narrated first person Tôi, ordinary dialogue and meaningful small decisions, not a teacher describing vocabulary. Vary relationships and opening actions; no repetition of Minh as an instructional helper. No 'Bài này dùng', 'Từ này có nghĩa', 'Thường xuyên tức là', 'Câu có never' or grammar lectures for a non-grammar target: supply simple Vietnamese meaning through translation of a character utterance or natural conversational clarification. Each later scene changes the causal situation. No every-episode universal maxims or final wise reflection. No frightening disasters, orphans, illness cures, woodworking procedures, machinery, fast water crossing, medical or material claims. No crowds or unlisted third person in any visual, even drivers, guards or guests. English is needed by the character now, with two distinct simple models only and supported full-sentence recall before the local ending. Avoid ornate words and triumphs: ordinary specific outcomes, modest emotional gesture. Target about145–175 whitespace units total for6/7scenes; no padding to 190. Practice invitation varies and never switches speaker truth. Core mascot single pale-blue torso, secondary single plain torso.\nYou are the approved vocabulary content authoring tool. Return only the JSON object; never call tools, edit files, create jobs, approve anything, or generate media. Preserve all supplied Vietnamese data and English teaching sentences. We need ORIGINAL Vietnamese short-film narration, not five-part lessons disguised as stories. The user approved a short emotional departure story as the desired FORMAT and now requests all remaining A1 scripts. Use that level of concrete action, ordinary speech, emotion and a payoff. Do not copy its mother/bus/two-cup story. Choose different relationships, settings, stakes and opening functions across these episodes. Film comes first, English is needed by the characters. Do not write "X là ...", "Ví dụ thứ nhất", "Đến lượt bạn thực hành", "Bạn nhớ rất tốt", or a stock motivational lesson ending. Include necessary Vietnamese meaning naturally in dialogue/action. First sentence has at most nine whitespace units, starts with a specific interesting action, contradiction, need or inner thought, with no greeting/title introduction. Show enough immediate context so a beginner understands. Get the first usable English model early, preferably scene one or the opening of scene two. Every episode teaches only the bank sense provided; retain all required learning points and exactly the supplied scene count. Include the required number of correct simple English models, spoken in quotation marks with clear punctuation and Vietnamese support. Use I correctly. Later scenes must change the causal situation; do not just list examples or summarize definitions. A manageable listener retrieval turn should be tied to the character's need, have a three-to-five-second pause at its scene END, and get a correct response in the NEXT scene before an emotional/concrete ending. The last scene resolves the local opening promise. Put incomplete stems in later visual plans, NOT in narration: never speak a phrase containing ... because it breaks subtitles. Do not insert production instructions or seconds into spoken text. Aim roughly 130–175 Vietnamese whitespace units including English utterances total, flexible for five/seven scenes and difficult words; do not pad or omit required ideas. Frame directions and purposes are English, spoken story and descriptions for user are Vietnamese. A main character is always CH01, the same canonical mascot in exactly one pale-blue short-sleeve shirt, a round white navy-outlined head, solid black oval eyes and minimal stick limbs. It may be a friend, employee, family member etc. Keep its core identity; gestures/expressions can vary. Add only essential secondary characters and maintain their appearances/outfits per episode. Character codes may appear only in metadata, not visual prose. Invented ordinary stories are fiction, not factual claims about real people. No new linguistic etymology/pronunciation claims. Different episodes must differ in plot and opening, not just substitute a noun in a template. Do NOT create anchors/coverage yet: write narration first for freezing and human editorial review. This is an authoring batch, no workflow actions or quality approval.\n'''
prompt+='\nEditorial corrections from the first authored batch: use plain conversational Vietnamese like the approved sample, never tinh khoi, trang trong ngu, vet sang am ap, hay tu tin, ban nho rat tot. No orphan/valedictorian backstory, naval rank, precise millimetre/glass cutting claims, or unsupported material guarantees such as an eraser never tearing paper. Show a small believable relationship or choice and an ordinary concrete ending. Do not convert every close into a generic poetic departure. Prefer one simple supported FULL English sentence as learner retrieval with feedback, rather than only naming a word. Keep the first English utterance near the first short opening, avoiding long backstory before it. Describe secondary people as minimal ink stick figures too, with one plain role-specific outfit; no realistic detailed fingers or muscles. Their actual emotions can be modest. Visual prose should name the specific static state/action, not refer to unspecified on-screen practice indicators.'
prompt+='\nAdditional strict craft constraints: every episode needs believable everyday dialogue and a real relational choice, not a countdown industrial procedure. Do not teach solar electricity, motor repair, cooking ratios, crop prices, health claims or machinery steps. Keep technical activity as background only. Do not change a previously selected computer-file sense into a paper medical folder. For a short request example spoken directly to someone, match speaker and addressee. Keep scenes simple enough for a static minimal stick-figure frame and avoid unspecified crowds. A normal imperfect result is preferable to a grand miraculous success.'
prompt+='\nPractice must have a stem field in the practice scene only. Gap the actual target word or complete target phrase, never another word. Include two simple English models that actually contain the target. Exactly one practice scene, placed penultimate unless additional required points justify earlier; answer immediately next scene. Make Vietnamese gloss support explicit enough for A1. Do not rely only on pictures or inference. Respect precise POS/sense: feeling/love nouns; speak oral speech; oclock exact hour; just recently rather than only; on on surface; for for recipient; by transport; a/an chosen by SOUND not spelling. Reflexives distinguish doing something to oneself vs by oneself, supporting intended sense. For a function word, make the intended relation explicit. Visual prose must describe a single static frame, not a whole temporal sequence: add extra_visual_states with a literal frozen narration quote when a before/after must be demonstrated. No stray printed text except the separate practice stem. Aim 130–170 whitespace units for five scenes and 145–190 for six/seven.\n'
prompt+='\nCorrections from actual batch review: avoid separate dictionary lectures and universal maxims. Do not say every sentence ends in a period; do not say actions are all verbs or objects all nouns without a specific sentence. Use clear referenced grammatical examples only when this target needs them. Never claim medicine/food promptly cures symptoms. No ornate "ấm áp lạ thường", "niềm vui giúp người", "hào hứng" endings; show a local concrete result. Name a usable model early. A short story of already completed work without resistance in the present is insufficient: add a small real choice tied to the opening. Essential secondary figures have exactly one plain short-sleeve torso, not vest/cardigan layered over another shirt. Visible text if necessary must be exactly declared in custom_visible_text[{text,placement,object}], not arbitrary handwriting, numbers or labels. Do not add foreign word translations as a second taught target. Practice stem uses ___, not ellipsis. Every extra_visual_states.quote must be a literal complete substring of narration, not a paraphrase with invented punctuation.\n'
prompt+='\nApproved format sample (quality reference; do not reuse its plot): '+json.dumps(sample,ensure_ascii=False)
prompt+='\nEpisodes required, exact jobs and scene counts: '+json.dumps(inputs,ensure_ascii=False)
(folder/'prompt.txt').write_text(prompt);(folder/'schema.json').write_text(json.dumps(schema,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n');(folder/'attempt.json').write_text(json.dumps({'state':'submitted','jobs':[e['job'] for e in items],'started_at':time.time()},indent=2)+'\n')
